      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The concentration-effect relationship of piritramide, a synthetic opioid analgesic predominantly used for postoperative analgesia and analgosedation, has not been reported so far."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty-four patients of both genders aged 58.1 (11.7) yr (mean (SD)) received inhalational anaesthesia for abdominal surgery. Postoperative pain was assessed with a visual analogue scale (VAS). Analgesia was provided with piritramide, infused at a rate of 7 micrograms.kg-1.min-1 until analgesia was considered sufficient (VAS < 25) or up to a maximum dose of 0.2 mg/kg. The plasma concentrations of piritramide were determined by gas chromatography. An inhibitory fractional sigmoid Emax-model was used to describe the relation between effect site concentration and perceived pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The equilibration half-life between plasma and effect site concentrations (T1/2 (keo)) was 16.8 min (median; range: 4.4-41.6 min). The steady-state plasma concentration required to produce 50% of maximum analgesia (EC50) was 12.1 ng/ml (range: 2.9-29.8 ng/ml) and correlated with initial pain intensity. The slope factor gamma was 1.9 (range: 0.5-6.1) and increased with age. Clinically relevant respiratory depression did not occur. Due to the relatively large equilibration half-life of the effect compartment, the context-sensitive half-time of the effect site concentrations after short-time administration (< 2 h) clearly exceeded those of alfentanil, sufentanil, and fentanyl."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The analgesic effect of piritramide was adequately described by an inhibitory fractional Emax-model. In order to overcome the pronounced hysteresis, piritramide should initially be administered as an intravenous bolus of at least 5 mg."
        }
      ]},
